STEP 6: hostPath stays on one node
----------------------------------
vi deploy-hostpath.yaml

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: hp
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels: {app: hp}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: {app: hp}
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: c
        image: busybox:1.36
        command: ["sh", "-c", "echo \"$(date) from $(hostname)\" >> /host/visits.txt; cat /host/visits.txt; sleep 3600"]
        volumeMounts:
        - name: h
          mountPath: /host
      volumes:
      - name: h
        hostPath:
          path: /tmp/hp-demo
          type: DirectoryOrCreate

k apply -f deploy-hostpath.yaml
k get pod -l app=hp -o wide
k logs deploy/hp

Restart the pod a few times and look at the logs:
  k rollout restart deploy/hp ; sleep 10 ; k logs deploy/hp
  k get pod -l app=hp -o wide

When the pod lands on the SAME node the file grows; on the OTHER
worker it starts over. Check both nodes:
  docker exec lab-worker  cat /tmp/hp-demo/visits.txt
  docker exec lab-worker2 cat /tmp/hp-demo/visits.txt


STEP 7: Why hostPath is dangerous
---------------------------------
Think: a pod that mounts hostPath "/" with root access can read
/etc/shadow and kubelet credentials of the node. Week 5 shows how
Pod Security Admission blocks this. Write your answer in Part C.


